<h2>Indian fertilizer inputs for export</h2>
<p>This category covers natural soil and crop-nutrition inputs sourced from India — neem cake, bio-fertilizers, bone meal and seaweed-based products. They serve horticulture, plantation agriculture and organic-input supply chains rather than the bulk synthetic fertilizer trade.</p>

<h2>Neem cake</h2>
<p>Neem cake is the solid residue after oil extraction from neem seeds. It supplies organic matter and nutrients, and its natural azadirachtin content gives it nematode- and pest-suppressant properties that make it valuable in horticulture and plantation crops. It's typically specified by oil content, moisture, mesh size and NPK values.</p>

<h2>Bio-fertilizers</h2>
<p>Bio-fertilizers contain living microbial cultures — nitrogen-fixing, phosphate-solubilising or potash-mobilising organisms depending on the product. Because the active component is alive, shelf life and storage conditions matter far more than with an inert input. Heat during transit degrades viability. Tell us your destination and expected transit time and we'll be honest about whether a given product will arrive in usable condition.</p>

<h2>Bone meal and seaweed-based fertilizers</h2>
<p>Bone meal is a slow-release phosphorus and calcium source; seaweed-based fertilizers supply trace elements and natural growth-promoting compounds. Both have established horticulture and specialty-agriculture demand.</p>

<h2>Import regulations come first</h2>
<p>Animal-origin fertilizer inputs — bone meal especially — face significant import controls in many markets, often requiring a prior permit and specific processing declarations. Plant-origin products like neem cake are usually simpler but still typically need a phytosanitary certificate.</p>
<p>Sort this out before anything else. {{ site.complianceChecklistCta }} See also our <a href="/products/organic-manure/">organic manure</a> range for composted animal-origin soil inputs.</p>

<h2>On certification claims</h2>
<p>We describe these products by what they are — natural, plant- or animal-origin inputs. We don't claim organic certification under any scheme. If your market requires certified product, raise it at the enquiry stage so we can be straightforward with you.</p>
